<body>

<!-- ══════════════════ PAGE 1 — ABSTRACT ══════════════════ -->
<section class="sheet">

  <p class="eyebrow">Technical Architecture Reference</p>
  <h1>Eye-Q</h1>
  <p class="standfirst">An open-vocabulary vision–language retrofit appliance that turns
  conventional IP cameras into cameras you can ask questions of, in plain English, with no
  model training and no cloud.</p>

  <p class="meta">Revision e57a6dd &nbsp;·&nbsp; branch feat/per-subject-tracking-and-vlm-load-control
  &nbsp;·&nbsp; 21 August 2026 &nbsp;·&nbsp; 8,793 LOC application / 3,790 LOC tests</p>

  <div class="abstract">
  <h2><span class="num">§0</span>Abstract</h2>

  <p class="lead">Conventional IP cameras detect motion; they cannot answer questions. Retrofitting
  semantic alerting onto them normally means training a bespoke detector for every condition — a
  hard-hat model, a spill model, a blocked-exit model — putting each new question behind its own
  labelling cycle. Eye-Q removes that cycle: an operator types the condition as a sentence,
  <em class="term">“Is this person bending over?”</em>, and a vision–language model answers it per
  subject, per frame, with no training and nothing leaving the device.</p>

  <p>The problem this creates is arithmetic. A VLM able to answer an arbitrary question about an
  image costs <em class="term">seconds</em> per query on edge CPUs, while a camera delivers thirty
  frames a second. Eye-Q resolves the mismatch with a two-stage cascade that arbitrages three orders
  of magnitude of latency: YOLOv8n runs continuously at ~16 ms per frame and does nothing but decide
  when reasoning is worth paying for; Moondream wakes only for the subjects that survive that gate.
  Between them sits a zero-copy shared-memory ring whose readers are lock-free and stale-tolerant,
  so the slow stage can lag seconds behind live without the fast stage ever blocking.</p>

  <p>The substance lies after the gate. Stage 1 assigns every detection a stable identity, so a
  frame of five workers yields five judgements rather than one — which multiplies inference demand
  by the size of the crowd and must therefore be paid for. Stage 2 pays with four ordered layers of
  load control — coalescing superseded triggers, memoising each subject’s verdict, prioritising
  subjects never yet seen, truncating the batch — all governed by one formula an operator can reason
  about: <code>duty = subjects × inference_s ÷ ttl</code>. Correctness measures carry equal weight:
  each crop is dimmed outside the subject’s own box so a neighbour cannot flip its verdict, and
  every crop is taken <em class="term">before</em> the first slow inference, so inference latency can
  never outlive the buffer it depends on.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="glance">
  <table>
    <tr><td class="k">Purpose</td><td>Plain-English alert rules over existing RTSP / USB / file sources; no per-condition training</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="k">Topology</td><td>One Python 3.13 process — four daemon threads plus an asyncio control plane</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="k">Two stages</td><td>YOLOv8n at ~16 ms/frame gates Moondream 2 B 4-bit at ~1.9 s/query (measured, Apple-Silicon MPS)</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="k">Transport</td><td>ZeroMQ PUB/SUB, two endpoints — <code>:5557</code> detections, <code>:5558</code> verdicts</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="k">Frame store</td><td>POSIX shared-memory ring — 150 slots, 414.7 MB at 1280×720, lock-free readers</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="k">Control plane</td><td>FastAPI + uvicorn, 17 routes, MJPEG preview, WebSocket broadcast; SQLite in WAL mode</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="k">Cloud dependency</td><td>None — all three VLM backends execute locally</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="k">Test suite</td><td>268 collected; 265 pass, 3 deselected as slow (23.1 s)</td></tr>
  </table>
  </div>

  <div class="note">
  <b>Scope and method.</b> Written against a complete read of the source at <code>e57a6dd</code>,
  with every file reference re-verified against the working tree. The seven diagram subjects
  originally scoped are consolidated into four figures to fit this format — deployment topology is
  carried by the process boundary in Figure 1, module and data-flow views fold into Figure 2, and
  the component inventory is given as tables. Claims the source could not substantiate are omitted;
  see the colophon on page 4.
  </div>

  <div class="footline"><span>Eye-Q — Technical Architecture Reference</span><span>Page 1 of 4</span></div>
</section>

<!-- ══════════════════ PAGE 2 — ARCHITECTURE ══════════════════ -->
<section class="sheet">

  <h2><span class="num">§1</span>System architecture</h2>

  <figure class="tight">
    {{FIG_1}}
    <figcaption><b>Figure 1 — System architecture.</b> One OS process. Capture writes into a
    shared-memory ring; Stage 1 reads the newest frame and decides only whether reasoning is
    warranted; Stage 2 wakes on that decision and fetches the pixels back by timestamp. The gate is
    drawn twice because <i>what to wake</i> and <i>what to ask</i> are separate concerns.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3>1.1 &nbsp;One process, four unequal threads</h3>
  <p>Everything runs in one process because of the ring buffer: a shared-memory frame store removes
  copies only if consumers reach it without serialising, and keeping both stages as threads in the
  owning process makes that free. The segments are nonetheless <em class="term">named</em> POSIX
  objects, so the multi-process seam exists when wanted. The threads are deliberately unequal —
  capture writes into a ring that overwrites rather than fills, Stage 1 is paced independently of
  capture because otherwise it starves the stage it exists to feed, and Stage 2 is kept entirely off
  the frame path.</p>

  <table>
    <tr><th style="width:28mm">Thread</th><th style="width:44mm">Owns</th><th style="width:28mm">Cadence</th><th>Under failure</th></tr>
    <tr><td class="k">uvicorn loop</td><td>Routes, <code>AlertSink</code>, WebSocket fan-out</td><td>request-driven</td><td>A malformed verdict is logged; the sink survives. JPEG encoding runs off-thread.</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="k">VideoCapture</td><td><code>cv2.VideoCapture</code>, ring writes</td><td><code>target_fps</code> or the file’s own FPS</td><td>Backoff 1 → 30 s over five attempts; files loop seamlessly.</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="k">YOLOWatchdog</td><td>Model, tracker, cooldown gate, overlay ring</td><td><code>watchdog_fps</code>, apart from capture</td><td>Warm-up failure falls back to CPU; repeated frames skipped by timestamp.</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="k">VLMWorker</td><td>Backend, verdict memo, both its ZMQ sockets</td><td>trigger-driven, 1 s idle poll</td><td>A blown timeout abandons the batch — one executor worker, so a hang blocks it.</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="k">VLMInfer pool</td><td>One in-flight inference</td><td>serial by design</td><td><code>future.result(timeout)</code> bounds the query and fails fast.</td></tr>
  </table>

  <h3>1.2 &nbsp;Two transport planes, single ownership</h3>
  <p>Detections travel on <code>:5557</code>, verdicts return on <code>:5558</code>. The split is
  not tidiness — ZeroMQ sockets are not thread-safe, so each publisher owns its socket in the thread
  that created it, and Stage 2 builds both of its own inside its worker loop. Sends are non-blocking
  behind a high-water mark: a slow consumer drops messages rather than stalling a producer. Ownership
  is likewise singular — the FastAPI lifespan builds every component in dependency order and tears it
  down in reverse, the fix for a defect where split ownership let the lifespan overwrite
  already-wired components and silently sever rule delivery to the VLM.</p>

  <div class="footline"><span>§1 &nbsp;System architecture</span><span>Page 2 of 4</span></div>
</section>

<!-- ══════════════════ PAGE 3 — WORKFLOW & AI ══════════════════ -->
<section class="sheet">

  <h2><span class="num">§2</span>End-to-end workflow and data flow</h2>

  <figure class="tight">
    {{FIG_2}}
    <figcaption><b>Figure 2 — From frame to alert.</b> Six stages and the three places a result
    lands. The return path is load-bearing: Stage 2 fetches the frame back out of the ring rather
    than carrying pixels forward, so the crop must finish before the ring wraps.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>Two invariants govern this path, each found by fixing the failure its absence causes.
  <strong>Crop before inference:</strong> inference takes seconds and the ring holds seconds, so
  fetching a frame just before each query leaves the second subject in a group with its frame already
  overwritten — invisible in a one-subject scene, total in a crowded one.
  <strong>Coalesce by subject, not label:</strong> keeping only the newest trigger per class discards
  four of five workers as “superseded” when they are different people. Rules flow the other way:
  <code>sync_rules()</code> hands Stage 1 the <em class="term">coarsest</em> filter satisfying every
  rule.</p>

  <h2 style="margin-top:5mm"><span class="num">§3</span>The AI/ML layer</h2>

  <figure class="tight">
    {{FIG_3}}
    <figcaption><b>Figure 3 — The Stage 2 decision path.</b> The left half decides whether a
    question is worth asking and runs before any pixels are touched; the memo there is the
    load control. Two contracts bind the language surface: “Yes” means violation, so prompts must be
    phrased that way round, and each rule asks a single predicate.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>Stage 1 is the motion filter, not the classifier: YOLOv8n can only ever say
  <em class="term">“person”</em> about a warehouse worker, and every question worth asking is asked
  by Stage 2 in English. Identity must be resolved before the gate can work, and is — a greedy IoU
  tracker matches boxes within a label across frames, chosen over ByteTrack because Ultralytics
  resolves its <code>lap</code> dependency at import time by attempting a runtime
  <code>pip install</code>, a hard failure offline. A swap under occlusion costs one redundant
  inference, never a missed alert.</p>

  <div class="footline"><span>§2–§3 &nbsp;Workflow and the AI/ML layer</span><span>Page 3 of 4</span></div>
</section>

<!-- ══════════════════ PAGE 4 — INNOVATION & LIMITS ══════════════════ -->
<section class="sheet">

  <h2><span class="num">§4</span>Innovation — what is genuinely hard here</h2>

  <figure class="tight">
    {{FIG_4}}
    <figcaption><b>Figure 4 — The twelve strongest ideas in the codebase,</b> grouped by the kind of
    win each buys and tagged with its source. Every one is running code at this revision.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>Two carry disproportionate weight. <strong>Four-layer load control with a published
  formula</strong> is what makes per-subject judging affordable rather than merely correct, and the
  status endpoint exposes <code>instances_judged</code>, <code>instances_memoised</code> and
  <code>instances_shed</code> so the arithmetic can be checked against reality.
  <strong>Focused crops</strong> are the subtlest: the padding that lets a VLM answer relational
  questions at all is the same padding that drags in the neighbours.</p>

  <h2 style="margin-top:3mm"><span class="num">§5</span>Deployment and security posture</h2>

  <table>
    <tr><th style="width:30mm">Concern</th><th>Position at this revision</th></tr>
    <tr><td class="k">Runtime</td><td><code>uv sync</code>, Python ≥ 3.13, one uvicorn process; weights fetched on first run. The device is probed MPS → CUDA → CPU <em>functionally</em> rather than by capability flag, because the flag lies. Stage 1 and an in-process VLM cannot both drive MPS &mdash; the process segfaults inside <code>_mps_convolution_impl</code> &mdash; so the two <code>auto</code>s are arbitrated in one place and Stage 2 wins: losing the accelerator costs Stage 1 9.9 ms/frame and would cost Stage 2 9.8 s/verdict.</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="k">VLM backends</td><td>Four behind one <code>Protocol</code>, one variable apart: <code>moondream4bit</code> in-process and probe-capable (default), <code>moondream</code> the fp32 baseline, <code>transformers</code> interruptible, <code>openai_compat</code> to a local llama.cpp / Ollama server.</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="k">Edge profile</td><td><code>.env.pi5</code> targets an 8 GB ARM board: <code>openai_compat</code>, 960×540, watchdog 4 FPS, 900 s TTL, batch 1 — ~3 GB of 8. <b>Unverified on hardware.</b></td></tr>
    <tr><td class="k">Network posture</td><td>No egress by default; the opt-in webhook is the only outbound path. <b>No authentication</b> — CORS <code>*</code>, bind <code>0.0.0.0:8000</code>, every mutating endpoint open. Trusted-LAN only.</td></tr>
  </table>

  <h2 style="margin-top:2.5mm"><span class="num">§6</span>Limitations</h2>

  <table>
    <tr><th style="width:46mm">Gap</th><th style="width:17mm">Severity</th><th>Consequence</th></tr>
    <tr><td class="k">No authentication or TLS</td><td>High</td><td>Anyone on the network can watch the feed, rewrite rules and read evidence.</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="k">No data retention policy</td><td>High</td><td><code>alerts</code> rows and snapshot JPEGs grow unbounded; only in-memory state is pruned.</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="k">Single camera; dashboard on public CDNs</td><td>Medium</td><td>One capture though the schema is multi-camera; Tailwind and fonts load remotely, so the UI is not air-gap clean.</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="k">No motion model; fire-and-forget webhooks; no ROI editor; dead surface</td><td>Low</td><td>~2 ids per person at 6 FPS — redundant inferences, never missed alerts. No webhook retry; <code>roi_json</code> undrawable; <code>SystemStatus</code> and <code>parse_with_confidence()</code> unused.</td></tr>
  </table>

  <div class="colophon">
  <b>Colophon and method.</b> Written against a full read of the source at <code>e57a6dd</code>, with
  every file reference re-verified against the working tree; the test figure is the actual output of
  <code>pytest -q -m "not slow"</code> — 265 passed, 3 deselected, 23.1 s. Figures are hand-authored
  vector SVG, generated by <code>docs/diagrams/build.py</code> and inlined.
  <b>Claims omitted:</b> the README states Stage 1 “filters 99%+ of frames” and that VLM utilisation
  drops “from 100% to &lt;1%”; neither is measured anywhere in the repository, so neither appears here
  — §4’s duty-cycle arithmetic is what the code implements, and where the README rounds the ring to
  “~405 MB” this uses the computed 414.7 MB. Two drifts noted: <code>demo_cctv.py</code> carries an
  uncommitted change contradicting its own docstring’s filename, and the pytest cache still lists a
  removed test file — hence 268 collected, not 311.
  </div>

  <div class="footline"><span>§4–§6 &nbsp;Innovation, deployment, limitations</span><span>Page 4 of 4</span></div>
</section>

</body>
</html>
